#show: setup.with(title: "Differential - M1 Problem statement", short: "M1 · Problem statement")

#title-block(
  title: [Differential: simulation-grounded AI diagnosis for analog audio electronics],
  subtitle: "Milestone 1 · Problem statement",
  meta: ("PRV 201 Frontiers of Technology (AI)", "Track A: Build", "Stevens Institute of Technology", "September 2026"),
)
*Executive summary.* When a tube preamplifier, studio console or vintage hi-fi unit fails, a technician must find which of its many parts failed, a paid bench evaluation at the specialist shops we sampled. For a circuit it has a model of (@fig:concept), Differential simulates how every cataloged part failure changes the readings, keeps a probability for every group of suspects that readings cannot tell apart, and recommends the measurement expected to narrow them most per unit of effort. A language model reads the complaint and explains; readings come from the technician; safety locks run in code. In a pilot on simulated units it named the group containing the failed part first on 95.6% of as-new and 93.2% of aged units, with 0.65 times half-split tracing's effort to a confirmed answer. The primary targets, fixed before any test unit is run (@tab:targets), include at least 4,728 of 5,000 simulated units right, 4,584 of 5,000 aged ones and 49 of 60 faults on real boards, plus margins over scripts and a chat model; each will be reported met or missed.

#figure(
  image("/results/figures/concept.svg", width: 100%),
  caption: [Offline, each circuit's faults are simulated with SPICE, a circuit simulator, under part tolerances, as-new and aged, and learned as fault signatures. Online, a Bayesian loop picks each measurement; a language model (LLM) reads the complaint and explains.],
) <fig:concept>

#part(1, "Problem, stakeholders and industry context", "MEMBER_1")

*The problem.* Studio consoles, tube preamplifiers and vintage hi-fi are repaired long after their makers stop supporting them, and finding the failed part is the slow step: U.S. defense maintainability guidance holds that most repair time typically goes to fault isolation \[1\], a premise the ticket coding below will test for audio benches. Differential, proposed here, helps a qualified bench technician find the part: it simulates the unit's circuit to rank the parts that could have failed and to choose each next measurement. Specialist shops we sampled charge \$125 to \$150 for a bench evaluation \[2, 3, 4\], and one bills an hour even when no fault is found \[5\]; these are examples, not a survey.

*Size of the need.* No statistic counts audio repair: its U.S. industry code was merged into a broader one in 2022 \[6\], and the closest occupation (24,720 people in May 2023) works mainly in retail and construction \[7, 8\]. The broader electronics repair group (117,700 jobs in 2025) is projected to grow 2% to 2035, below the 3.5% average \[9, 10\]. Only press reports speak for the specialist niche: skilled hi-fi technicians "are scarce, and their numbers are dwindling" \[11\], and a Chicago shop for old audio gear shrank from seven technicians to two \[12\]. An AI assistant raised support agents' productivity 15%, most for the least experienced \[13\], a case for supporting newer technicians, not replacing them.

*Where it pays.* A model needs the schematic transcribed (we will time one) and, for our 59-part channel strip, 81 minutes of computer time, so it pays where one model serves many units, such as a console of identical channel strips.

*Where current AI stands.* Maintenance AI works from records and documents (service histories \[14\], manuals \[15\], repair guidance paired with failure prediction \[16\], agentic workflows \[17\], repair guides \[18\]); none of these five simulates the circuit. The best chat model scores 95.50% on textbook analog questions, graded by another language model \[19\], but at the bench parts sit anywhere within tolerance, faults look alike, measurements cost effort and some points carry lethal voltage. A pipeline that added a circuit-diagram detector and a simulator check to a language model lifted it from 79.52% to 97.59% on undergraduate circuit problems \[20\]; Differential brings such a check to diagnosis.

*Jurisdiction and rules.* We design for the U.S. (the team is in New Jersey) and check EU rules for later sale. No federal AI statute regulates such a tool, but the FTC has acted against unsupported AI accuracy claims \[21\]. Texas's AI act (in force since 2026) binds it only through bans on intentional misuse, such as inciting harm or discriminating \[22\]; Colorado's covers automated decisions about people, such as employment, from 2027 \[23\], one reason our terms of use forbid rating staff; and the Administration has asked Congress to preempt state AI laws it deems unduly burdensome \[24\]. The EU AI Act would not deem a bench aid high-risk unless shops rated staff or trainees with it \[25\]; users must be told they deal with an AI from 2 August 2026 \[26\], and the 2026 amendments keep the duty to support staff's AI literacy \[27\].

