*Interviews before M2*, after an ethics-board determination: six 45-minute interviews (three technicians from independent audio shops, a trainee, a shop owner, a vintage-gear owner), recruited through audio societies and trade contacts, never an employer, on diagnosis time, recurring faults, judging readings, what a tool may do and consent to removals; and 50 anonymized tickets from one shop, with its technicians' consent, coded for faults and time to find them.

#decision("The primary user is a qualified bench technician")[
*Options:* a consumer self-repair assistant or a professional bench tool. *Choice:* professional: diagnosis often means testing a powered, open chassis, and 5 of the channel strip's 23 test points can exceed 50 V (@tab:library). OSHA requires employers to de-energize live parts unless testing needs power (parts under 50 V excepted if that adds no burn or arc-blast risk) and to let only qualified persons work on or test them \[28, 29, 30\]; the self-employed fall outside the OSH Act \[31, 32\], so our terms of use ask it of every user. *Consequence:* offered to repair businesses; trainees supervised; mains-side work refused. Hobbyists keep forums and chat assistants, which do not know which points of their unit carry high voltage.
]

#part(2, "How diagnosis is done today, and the data landscape", "MEMBER_2")

*Today's practice* (the interviews will check it): with a multimeter, an oscilloscope, a signal generator and a variac or series lamp \[33, 34\], a technician confirms the complaint; looks for burnt or bulging parts; checks the supplies; follows a test tone stage by stage, often halving the search ("half-split tracing"); compares readings with the manual's voltage chart or the healthy channel \[35\]; tests tubes and electrolytics (tube tester, ESR meter); unsolders a suspect and confirms the fault clears. In our reading, the time goes to judging which reading is really "off", since parts sit anywhere within tolerance and aging electrolytics lose capacitance and gain series resistance \[36\], and to unsoldering suspects that prove good; the ticket coding will measure it. Replacing every electrolytic ("recapping") often cures a symptom but costs parts and time, risks the board and never names the failed part.

*The information is uneven.* Manuals with schematics and voltage charts exist for many classic models, but units are often modified or undocumented. U.S. repair laws cover newer digital products, not vintage analog gear: New York's took effect in December 2023 \[37, 38\], and six other states followed with limits by date or product \[39, 40, 41, 42, 43, 44\]; the EU's obligation to repair covers only its Annex II products, such as washing machines \[45, 46\]. So Differential needs a model per product, built from the schematic, and trusts its hazard map (the points that can exceed 50 V) only once the model's healthy readings match the manual's chart; until then every point of a high-voltage unit counts as high voltage. "No single fault fits" covers modified units; units without a schematic are out of scope.

*Labeled fault data is scarce, so we simulate it.* Confirming a failed part takes unsoldering and is rarely recorded; shop logs are private and, after recapping, unlabeled. We know of no public dataset of bench readings with confirmed failed parts; published methods use benchmark filters \[47\] and simulated data \[48\]. Analog-test research built simulated fault dictionaries \[49\], found faults the test points cannot tell apart (ambiguity groups) \[50\] and applied machine learning \[51\]; we follow it (@tab:library). A simulated unit draws every part within its tolerance, the mains within ±5% (our assumption), and one cataloged fault (an open, short, drift, or a mode such as a leaky transistor or worn tube). Since units reaching a bench are old, 50% of training units are also aged: electrolytics up to 30% low with up to 3× the series resistance (past the usual end-of-life limits of 20% and 2× \[36\]), resistors up to 10% high, tubes up to 30% weaker. Each reading carries instrument error: a 10 MΩ meter loads each DC node \[52\] and reads within ±(0.5% + 2 counts) \[53\], an oscilloscope within ±3% of full scale \[54\]. The library holds 253,000 units (0 failed to converge). Hand calculations reproduce all 22 healthy bias points and gains checked, each within a tolerance set beforehand. For faults, an audit re-solved 53 randomly drawn faults (up to two per fault type and circuit) with a solver we wrote from the published device equations, without the simulator: all 53 agreed within twice the meter's accuracy (95% interval 93%-100%), after a first run in which 6 of 42 disagreed through the audit solver's own omissions. That checks the simulator, not reality; the boards test reality. Static simulation cannot produce intermittent or mechanical faults, and the catalog holds single faults, so those are out of scope or flagged. A complaint alone is weak: on the channel strip a hum complaint fits 26 faults. So labels stay scarce, readings are ambiguous within tolerance and a complaint narrows little: a tool must learn from simulation and choose its measurements.

