#decision("Original generic circuits instead of commercial schematics")[
*Options:* model a commercial product from its service manual, or design original circuits with the same topologies. *Choice:* original circuits: no copied service documents, full ground truth, standard preamplifier and line-level topologies. *Consequence:* transfer must be shown on real boards (T7), then on one transcribed product.
]

#part(3, "Objectives and measurable success criteria", "MEMBER_3")

*Terms.* The engine: the Bayesian diagnoser. The reader: what turns a complaint into symptoms (rules offline, the language model live). A lock: a step withheld until the technician records a precondition. The ticket: the written diagnosis record.

*Objectives.* (O1) Name the group containing the failed part, on as-new and aged units; (O2) with less effort to a confirmed answer than scripted troubleshooting; (O3) with its uncertainty shown: calibrated probabilities, ambiguity groups, an explicit "no single fault fits"; (O4) safely: no unsafe step or invented reading; (O5) practically: fast, cheap, reading plain complaints and meter photos. T1-T7 test O1, T8-T11 O2, T12-T14 and T22-T23 O3, T15-T17 O4, T18-T21 O5; @tab:targets lists the primaries and gates, the supplement the secondary targets (reported, not deciding success).

*Effort.* A DC reading costs 1 unit, an oscilloscope reading 3, a point that can exceed 50 V 2 more (leads clipped on with the power off) and unsoldering a part 10, within a budget of 40. Every method is charged one unsoldering test to confirm its named suspect unless it already confirmed one (a wrong answer's test exposes it; the recovery is not counted). Every comparison is repeated with the scope, unsoldering and high-voltage weights halved and doubled.

