*How it will be measured.* The test set holds 5,000 single-fault units (2,500 channel strip, 500 per block) and 5,000 aged units with the same mix, on seeds disjoint from training (aging within the training ranges; harsher aging meets the tool only in the tolerance curve and on the boards). Faults count by how often they cause a symptom, since a unit without one is redrawn. Complaints come from a phrase bank the reader was not developed on (its score was seen once, before a rule change; T18 uses a sealed bank), in three voices (a bench technician, a hobbyist owner, a studio client), never naming a part, each symptom dropped with probability 20% and others added with 2%. Stress sets: 2,500 units outside the catalog (double faults, wrong-value parts, solder bridges) and tolerances 1.5-3 times wider (a curve). Baselines: a fixed-order service chart, half-split tracing (halving the signal path with a tone; the strongest script) and random probing (never unsolders) share the engine's inference and stopping rule, and the scripts unsolder their leading suspect at 50% belief; a language model, alone and with our simulator as a tool, picks from the same list on 300 stratified units and stops when it names an answer. A healthy-channel comparison, usual in stereo gear, joins them at M2 without a bar (it needs twin units on a shared supply). For T7 a teammate who does not record draws 60 buildable faults at random and inserts them in sealed order into three 24 V line-driver boards, one built from used parts; each point is recorded once per fault and every method is replayed on the recordings. Study S1 will time at least six technicians with and without the tool, without a bar (six cannot show a benefit reliably); release waits if most follow its planted wrong step without checking.

*How the bars were set; no test unit has been run.* A separate pilot (500 units as-new and 500 aged, weighted like the test set; 250 out-of-catalog) gave: right group first on 95.6% of as-new and 93.2% of aged units, a 2.4-point cost of aging (5.2% flagged, 1.6% misnamed). To a confirmed answer, the engine alone spent 16.0 units (6.1 probing, 9.9 unsoldering) and half-split tracing 24.6 (13.5 and 11.1); the engine asked for 0.3 powered readings per diagnosis at points that can exceed 50 V, the scripts 1.8-1.9 (no bar). Each bar is the pilot estimate moved 1.5 standard errors against the system and rounded, never past the estimate; with test sets 10 times the pilot, a system as good as in the pilot passes each primary with probability 81-88%. T5 has no pilot (no API key): +10 points is the smallest margin we judged worth a model per circuit over a general one. T7's 70% is what 60 faults can show: a pass needs 49 right, which a system truly 90% accurate reaches 98% of the time, an 85% one 82% and a 70% one 3%. Each primary is judged alone; if independent, all six pilot-estimable primaries would all pass with a chance near 36%, and about one is expected to miss; every miss will be reported.

*Scope.* In: single static faults (opens, shorts, drift, wear) and the hum and distortion they cause, in supplies, preamplifier and line-level stages. Out (non-goals): power amplifiers, intermittent and mechanical faults, double faults (flagged only), unpowered checks and visual inspection (not yet modeled), consumer self-repair, mains-side work, unattended probing. *Plan:* interviews and ticket coding by M2 (week 8); the boards, T7 and the full evaluation by M3 (week 11); S1 and the live runs by M5 (week 13). If the boards or the ethics review slip, T7 or S1 is reported as pending, never replaced.

#decision("Score by ambiguity group and effort to a confirmed answer")[
*Options:* per-part accuracy and a count of measurements, or group accuracy and weighted effort to a confirmed answer. *Choice:* the latter: per-part scoring rewards lucky guesses between faults with identical readings, unsoldering costs more than probing, and an answer is worth acting on only once confirmed. *Consequence:* group sizes and raw effort are also reported.
]

#part(4, "Preliminary AI approach, risks and ethics", "MEMBER_4")

*Why a hybrid.* Language models guess when uncertain, partly because training and evaluation reward guessing \[55\]. Alone, a model has no expected readings for this unit under tolerance, no calibrated belief over hundreds of suspects and no cost per measurement; with a simulator as a tool \[20\] it checks one guess at a time but lacks the last two (T5 and the model-with-simulator comparison test this). Simulation supplies the physics; Bayesian inference keeps a probability for every suspect group, updated after each reading through likelihoods learned from simulated units: one to three full-covariance Gaussians per fault (chosen by the Bayesian information criterion) over the circuit's readings, giving a likelihood for any subset of readings in closed form. "No single fault fits" is a hypothesis with a broad flat density, set on validation units to flag at most 5% of single faults. Boosted trees \[56\] are a comparison engine (pilot top-1 91.8% against 94.3%). The next measurement maximizes expected information gain per unit of effort, the one-step entropy rule of model-based diagnosis \[57\] with a cost term (an optimal sequence needs a search \[58\]); a diagnosis stops at 90% belief in one group, at the budget, or when nothing informative remains. The language model (Anthropic's Claude, version pinned) reads complaints (offline, rules do), proposes meter-photo readings for confirmation (offline, image processing does) and explains; every number it writes must trace to a reading (T17), and it stays only if T5, T18 and T19 show it helps.

*Set aside:* consistency-based candidates \[57\] (tolerances blur conflicts); retrieval over manuals (they describe healthy units); hand-built troubleshooting networks (one per product); deep networks that condition on any subset of readings \[59, 60\] and choose measurements by information gain \[61\], but are harder to audit than one density per fault.

*Autonomy.* Levels, by the functions automated \[62\]: L0, none; L1, it ranks suspects; L2, it also recommends each step; L3, it takes readings with approval; L4, unattended. Differential is L2: a person takes and confirms every reading.

*Safety.* A hazard map lists every test point that can exceed 50 V, healthy or faulted, and a fault can put high voltage where there is normally none: channel-strip point TP10, normally about 0 V, reaches 126 V under "C203 short circuit". Steps at points above 50 V carry hands-off and discharge warnings; in a high-voltage chassis every powered step says "high voltage inside", names a meter rated CAT II 600 V or better (IEC 61010) and waits until the technician records how the unit was brought up (variac, series lamp, current-limited supply, or already running). Before a part is removed, the owner's approval is recorded and the technician records under 2 V at every point that can hold high voltage; the check lapses after 5 minutes without a bleeder, as a discharged capacitor recovers charge \[36\], and on any power-up. These are checks, not guarantees: the locks act on typed readings, so they bound what the tool recommends and records, not what a person does; the residual shock risk rests on the user's qualification, and the hazard map covers only the modeled circuit.

*People.* Runner-up suspects and reasons are shown, yet AI can mislead: consultants given GPT-4 were 19 percentage points less likely to solve a task outside its competence \[63\], and those trusting AI more report less critical thinking \[64\]. Trainees commit their own next step before seeing the tool's and need a named supervisor near high voltage; a tutor giving hints instead of answers largely mitigated the learning loss an unrestricted chat model caused \[65\]. S1 plants one wrong recommendation on a non-hazardous step of a 24 V board, after an ethics-board determination; participants are told beforehand that some advice may be wrong, are debriefed and recruited outside their employers. Whether owners turn faster diagnosis into a faster pace is a question for the interviews. As fairness audits do for groups of people \[66\], errors will be broken down by complaint voice, since owners who describe faults in plain words could get worse diagnoses (non-English complaints are not covered); breakdowns by fault type and circuit show testability, not fairness. A gap above 5 points on a primary whose 95% interval excludes zero blocks release until explained.

*Accountability.* The team answers for the hazard map, stated performance and corrections; a shop, for its circuit models and staff; the technician, for measurements and precautions. Responsibility should follow control, lest the technician become a moral crumple zone, blamed for a system they cannot steer \[67\]: the ticket records what the tool recommended, why, and what the technician attested. Whether advice software is a product (strict liability) or a service (negligence) is unsettled in the U.S.; bills would make AI systems products \[68, 69\] or shield developers when licensed professionals use them \[70\]; the revised EU directive makes software placed on the market after 9 December 2026 a product \[71\]. California bars anyone who developed or used an AI from answering a harm claim by saying the AI acted autonomously \[72\].

